delta_low_at: do not let one store claim the first byte of the next

The language's stores of bytes are copied into the low arena and an address in
the program is translated to the address of its copy. A rule may name the byte
after a store, so the lookup counts one past the end as inside -- but it does
that in the same pass as the ordinary case, first match winning.

Where the linker puts two stores next to each other, the first byte of the
second store is also one past the end of the first. Whichever was registered
first then claims it, and the answer is a pointer just past that store's copy
in the arena rather than the copy of the store the address is actually in. What
comes back is whatever the arena happens to hold there.

On x86 the stores this engine has do not land adjacently, so nothing shows. On
aarch64 they do. The engine still spoke most text, with slightly different
samples, and spoke nothing at all for words whose rules read a constant through
a misresolved store -- "seven." and "eight." each gave a wave file with no
samples in it -- and faulted in the pitch rules on others, with a null node
reference in visleft.

Looking for a store the address is properly inside before allowing the
one-past-the-end case settles it, because an address one past the end of one
store and inside another belongs to the one it is inside. With this, an
aarch64 build answers byte for byte what an x86 build answers.

diff --git a/src/delta/delta_low.c b/src/delta/delta_low.c
index 9d04a63..a7ab30a 100644
--- a/src/delta/delta_low.c
+++ b/src/delta/delta_low.c
@@ -110,9 +110,13 @@ void *delta_low_at(const void *p)
         return 0;

     for (i = 0; i < regions; i++)
-        if (c >= region[i].at && c <= region[i].at + region[i].bytes)
+        if (c >= region[i].at && c < region[i].at + region[i].bytes)
             return region[i].copy + (c - region[i].at);

+    for (i = 0; i < regions; i++)
+        if (c == region[i].at + region[i].bytes)
+            return region[i].copy + region[i].bytes;
+
     fprintf(stderr, "evv: %p is in the program and in none of the stores"
             " copied out of it\n", p);
     abort();
